																<div id=3D"tab-opinion-5178330" class=3D"hidden-content">
						<div class=3D"-display-inline-block text-left">
														=09

															SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES<p></p>

															_________________<p></p>

															No. 24=E2=80=93724<p></p>

															_________________<p></p>

															THE HAIN CELESTIAL GROUP, INC., et&nbsp;al.,
PETITIONERS <em>v.</em> SARAH PALMQUIST, individually and as next
friend of E. P., a minor, et&nbsp;al.<p></p>

															on writ of certiorari to the united states
court of appeals for the fifth circuit<p></p>

															[February 24, 2026]<p></p>

															Justice Thomas, concurring.<p></p>

															I join the Court=E2=80=99s opinion in full. I write
separately to note my skepticism of the doctrine of =E2=80=9Cimproper
joinder,=E2=80=9D which the District Court invoked to dismiss Whole Foods
in this case. The doctrine appears to allow federal courts to
enlarge their jurisdiction by assessing the merits of claims over
which they lack jurisdiction.<p></p>

															=E2=80=9CFederal courts are courts of limited
jurisdiction.=E2=80=9D <em>Kokkonen</em> v. <em>Guardian Life Ins. Co. of
America</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/=
cases/federal/us/511/375/">511 U.S.
375</a>, 377 (1994). As =E2=80=9Can essential ingredient of separation and
equilibration of powers,=E2=80=9D the Constitution gives Congress broad
power over the scope of federal courts=E2=80=99 jurisdiction. <em>Steel
Co</em>. v. <em>Citizens for Better Environment</em>, <a class=3D"related-c=
ase" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/523/83/">523 U.S.
83</a>, 101 (1998). =E2=80=9CWhen we assume jurisdiction too hastily, we
risk aggrandizing ourselves at the expense of the political
branches.=E2=80=9D <em>Monsalvo</em> <em>Velazquez</em> v. <em>Bondi</em>, =
604
U.S. 712, 737 (2025) (Thomas, J., dissenting). Accordingly, if a
federal court determines that it lacks jurisdiction in a case, =E2=80=9Cthe
only function remaining to the court is that of announcing the fact
and dismissing the cause.=E2=80=9D <em>Ex parte McCardle</em>, 7 Wall. 506,
514 (1869).<p></p>

															Federal courts therefore =E2=80=9Cmust determine that
they have jurisdiction before proceeding to the merits.=E2=80=9D
<em>Lance</em> v. <em>Coffman</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https=
://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/549/437/">549 U.S.
437</a>, 439 (2007) (<em>per&nbsp;curiam</em>). =E2=80=9CThe requirement th=
at
jurisdiction be established as a threshold matter spring[s] from
the nature and limits of the judicial power of the United States
and is inflexible and without exception.=E2=80=9D <em>Steel</em> <em>Co</em=
>.,
523 U.&nbsp;S., at 94=E2=80=9395 (internal quotation marks omitted). =E2=80=
=9CFor a
court to pronounce upon=E2=80=9D the merits =E2=80=9Cwhen it has no jurisdi=
ction to
do so is, by very definition, for a court to act ultra vires.=E2=80=9D
<em>Id</em>., at 101=E2=80=93102.<p></p>

															In this case, federal jurisdiction was based on
diversity of citizenship. The removing defendant, Hain Celestial
Group, invoked federal jurisdiction under 28 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C.
=C2=A71332(a), which gives district courts jurisdiction over =E2=80=9Ccivil
actions where the matter in controversy exceeds the sum or value of
$75,000 .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. and is between .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. citizens of
different States.=E2=80=9D This Court has always interpreted =C2=A71332(a)=
=E2=80=99s
language =E2=80=9Cto require =E2=80=98complete diversity,=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=
=80=9D meaning that a
federal court can exercise jurisdiction only if no plaintiff shares
state citizenship with any defendant. <em>Carden</em> v. <em>Arkoma
Associates</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.c=
om/cases/federal/us/494/185/">494 U.S.
185</a>, 187 (1990) (citing <em>Strawbridge</em> v. <em>Curtiss</em>, 3
Cranch 267 (1806)).<p></p>

															Although the parties in this case were not
completely diverse, the District Court exercised jurisdiction based
on the doctrine of =E2=80=9Cimproper joinder.=E2=80=9D Under that doctrine,=
 federal
courts exercise jurisdiction over cases that originally involved
nondiverse defendants by first deeming the claims against those
nondiverse defendants sufficiently weak on the merits and then
dismissing them. See, <em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., <em>International Energy
Ventures Mgmt., L.L.C.</em> v. <em>United Energy Group, Ltd.</em>, 818
F.3d 193, 205=E2=80=93209 (CA5 2016). Here, the Palmquists, Texas citizens,
sued both an out-of-state defendant, Hain Celestial Group, and a
Texas defendant, Whole Foods. The District Court therefore lacked
jurisdiction over the case when it was removed to federal court.
The District Court nonetheless proceeded to dismiss Whole Foods
based on its merits assessment that there was not a =E2=80=9Creasonable
basis to predict that [the Palmquists] could recover from Whole
Foods=E2=80=9D under Texas products-liability law. App. to Pet. for Cert.
25a=E2=80=9326a. After dismissing Whole Foods, it adjudicated the claims
against Hain Celestial on the merits.<p></p>

															The Fifth Circuit vacated that merits judgment
for lack of jurisdiction, but not because it thought that
conducting an improper-joinder inquiry was inappropriate. 103
F.&nbsp;4th 294, 302=E2=80=93307 (2024). It simply thought that the
Palmquists possibly stated meritorious claims against Whole Foods
under Texas law. See <em>id</em>., at 305=E2=80=93307. It reaffirmed that t=
he
improper-joinder doctrine allows federal courts to engage in a
=E2=80=9CRule 12(b)(6)-type analysis=E2=80=9D of claims against nondiverse
defendants, albeit with legal ambiguities resolved in the
plaintiffs=E2=80=99 favor. <em>Id</em>., at 306. Federal Rule of Civil
Procedure 12(b)(6) dismissals, of course, are merits
determinations. See <em>Federated Department Stores, Inc.</em> v.
<em>Moitie</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.c=
om/cases/federal/us/452/394/">452 U.S.
394</a>, 399, n.&nbsp;3 (1981).<p></p>

															Other federal courts adopt a similar approach to
dismiss claims against nondiverse parties. See, <em>e.g.</em>,
<em>Johnson</em> v. <em>Midwest Div.</em>-<em>RBH, LLC</em>, 88
F.&nbsp;4th 731, 735=E2=80=93736 (CA8 2023); <em>Jerome-Duncan, Inc.</em> v=
.
<em>Auto-By-Tel, L. L. C</em>., <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://l=
aw.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/F3/176/904/596878/">
176 F.3d 904</a>, 907=E2=80=93909 (CA6 1999); <em>Legg</em> v. <em>Wyeth</e=
m>,
<a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appe=
llate-courts/F3/428/1317/565315/">
428 F.3d 1317</a>, 1324, 1320=E2=80=931325 (CA11 2005). Courts justify this
approach based on a group of this Court=E2=80=99s early-20th-century
precedents that recognized a doctrine called fraudulent joinder.
See, <em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., <em>Alabama Great Southern R. Co.</em> v.
<em>Thompson</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia=
.com/cases/federal/us/200/206/">200 U.S.
206</a>, 217 (1906).[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us=
/607/24-724/#F2" name=3D"T2" id=3D"T2">2</a>]*<p></p>

															But this Court=E2=80=99s precedents do not support the
lower courts=E2=80=99 more recent approach. As I read them, this Court=E2=
=80=99s
=E2=80=9Cfraudulent joinder=E2=80=9D precedents concerned only whether the
plaintiff avoided federal diversity jurisdiction by bad faith or
actual fraud=E2=80=94such as lying about a party=E2=80=99s conduct or citiz=
enship.
See <em>Alabama Great Southern R. Co.</em>, 200 U.&nbsp;S., at 218
(asking whether the plaintiff =E2=80=9Cattempt[ed] to commit a fraud upon
the jurisdiction of the Federal courts=E2=80=9D); <em>Illinois Central
R.&nbsp;Co.</em> v. <em>Sheegog</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"htt=
ps://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/215/308/">215 U.S.
308</a>, 316 (1909) (asking whether a claim was =E2=80=9Cfraudulent and
false=E2=80=9D); <em>Mecom</em> v. <em>Fitzsimmons Drilling Co.</em>, <a cl=
ass=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/28=
4/183/">284 U.S.
183</a>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases=
/federal/us/284/190/">190</a>
(1931) (asking whether the plaintiff committed =E2=80=9Cactual fraud=E2=80=
=9D).
These precedents were limited to cases of =E2=80=9Cextreme abuse.=E2=80=9D
<em>Murriel-Don Coal Co.</em> v. <em>Aspen Ins. UK Ltd</em>., 790 F.
Supp. 2d 590, 596 (ED Ky. 2011) (Thapar, J.). Unlike the lower
courts=E2=80=99 more recent decisions, they did not appear to turn on the
strength of the claims on the merits.<p></p>

															Federal courts sitting in diversity likely
cannot dismiss nondiverse parties based on their view of the merits
of the claims against those parties. Doing so appears unfaithful to
Congress=E2=80=99s limits on our diversity jurisdiction and inconsistent
with this Court=E2=80=99s precedents. As Judge Thapar has put it, the
improper-joinder doctrine =E2=80=9Crequires federal courts to exercise
jurisdiction where none exists over questions of state law that the
state courts are better suited to address themselves.=E2=80=9D <em>Id.</em>=
,
at 597. In a future case where the issue is briefed and squarely
presented, this Court should consider the propriety of the
improper-joinder doctrine.<p></p>
<div class=3D"opinion-footnotes">
<strong class=3D"heading-5 font-w-bold">Notes</strong>
<div class=3D"opinion-footnote"><span class=3D"opinion-footnote-ref"><a hre=
f=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-724/#T2" name=3D"F2=
" id=3D"F2">2</a></span>
<span class=3D"opinion-footnote-text">*Many Circuits still use the
=E2=80=9Cfraudulent joinder=E2=80=9D term today, even when they engage in t=
he same
merits analysis that the Fifth Circuit more accurately calls
improper joinder. See, <em>e.g</em>., <em>Johnson</em>, 88 F.&nbsp;4th,
at 735=E2=80=93736 (holding that =E2=80=9Cjoinder .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. was fraud=
ulent=E2=80=9D
because state-law claim was unlikely to succeed on the merits);
<em>Jerome-Duncan, Inc.</em>, 176 F.&nbsp;3d, at 907=E2=80=93909 (affirming
=E2=80=9Cfraudulent joinder=E2=80=9D dismissal because of weakness of claim=
 under
state law); <em>Cline</em> v. <em>Dart Transit Co</em>., 804 Fed. Appx.
307, 312 (CA6 2020) (holding that =E2=80=9Cfraudulent joinder occurred=E2=
=80=9D
because plaintiff lacked =E2=80=9Ca colorable cause of
action=E2=80=9D).</span></div>
</div>

